From ff1a4fc7f9ff0ba0f1b0a3d0d02c9f27d3655e63 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Corey <coreysho@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 06:15:50 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] The Barrows puzzle is pictures, and the chest stops crashing
 on close

TWO THINGS, one of them a class of bug.

THE CRASH. Looting the chest and closing the window threw
"inv_moveitem $inv requires protected access: barrows_reward_store" and
disconnected the player. Player.closeModal runs an [if_close] script with
executeScript(script, FALSE) - the second argument is `protect`, so an
if_close handler has NO protected access; the client is what asked for the
close and the server may be in the middle of anything. barrows_reward_store
and bank are both protect=yes (InvType's default) and INV_MOVEITEM checks
BOTH ends, so the first move threw.

Player.processQueue runs a queued script with executeScript(script, TRUE), so
the banking is queued and happens a tick later. The trailing 0 is the unused
int argument a no-parameter queue takes, as the thieving stalls do it.

No loot was lost: barrows_reward_store is scope=perm and
~barrows_chest_search flushes the store before the next chest rolls. That
backstop was written for the disconnect case and caught the one nobody
expected.

protect=no on the store would not have been enough and would have been worse
anyway - the other end is the BANK, which is exactly the inv that should
require protected access.

Every [if_close] in the tree was swept, following ~proc and @label calls.
Eight reach an inv write; seven are fine because the invs they touch are
protect=no (tradeoffer, inv, skillguide - trade, duel, party chest, skill
guide). Barrows was the only one writing a protected inv.

The battery asserted '~barrows_reward_flush;' in the if_close block - it
checked for exactly the thing that was broken. It now asserts the opposite
from both sides, plus the queue, plus the flush-before-roll backstop.

THE PUZZLE. Old School's last door asks a sequence puzzle: three symbols and
a question mark, three candidates, click the one that completes it. This had
the same puzzle in words, on the stated grounds that sprite art "could be
added but never rendered or verified" here.

THAT WAS FALSE. The sandbox cannot PACK the media archive (jimp is a stub),
but sprites live in the repo as loose PNG sheets in sprites/ with a
meta/<name>.opt for the tile size, packClientMedia just scans sprites/*.png
with no pack file to register, and tools/ifrender.py reads those PNGs
directly. A round was deferred on a premise one `ls sprites/` would have
corrected.

tools/genbarrowspuzzle.py draws 22 tiles with PIL into
sprites/barrows_puzzle.png - 192x128, 32x32 tiles, two colours - in five
families: a square filling by quarters, polygons of 3-7 sides, a widening
ellipse, a growing bar, a shrinking circle. 0xFF00FF is palette index 0, so
that is the background; the shapes have hard edges because antialiasing would
spend hundreds of palette slots on near-greys and muddy them in an indexed
palette anyway.

Drawing rather than importing is what lets the puzzle SET be chosen here too,
and the generator refuses to run if a puzzle offers its answer twice, has
duplicate candidates, or if any slot is the answer in fewer than two of the
eight.

There is no if_setgraphic in this engine - a graphic component's sprite is
fixed at pack time - so all eight puzzles are built into the interface, one
LAYER each, and ~barrows_puzzle_show hides the seven not being asked;
if_sethide only works on layers. That proc is hide-all-then-show-one in two
straight runs, because nothing can compute a component and `$n ! 0` is not an
expression in this language - `!` is only a comparison inside an if. rs2check
accepts it and the server compiler does not.

if_addresumebutton + p_pausebutton suspends the script inside the [oploc1]
that clicked the door, so it keeps its loc context and its protected access
and resumes with last_com. Closing the window returns -1 rather than a wrong
answer, and the gate returns without shifting the tunnels - a misclick on
Close must not re-lay the maze.

Verification: clean build; rs2check 1703 scripts / 493 configs, 0 ERROR
0 CHECK; barrows_battery.py 979 checks (917 before); barrows_mutate.py 237
mutations, every one caught by its own check; every other battery and sim
still green.

Four faults found while verifying, all worth the record:
  - a comment added to barrows_chest.rs2 mentioned ~barrows_chest_search by
    name, and an existing raw-text check found the comment instead of the
    call - green with the call deleted. Every source grep in that battery now
    goes through nocomment(). Same fault as the four slices that swallowed
    the next block's comments, from the other direction.
  - .index() on a string a mutation removes RAISES, which the harness reports
    as a non-zero exit with no check named. A crash is not a catch, for the
    tenth time in this project; it is .find() now.
  - a candidate component was checked for its graphic= value, so a mutation
    that made it a type=rect left that line sitting there unread.
  - the spec's own `slot` field was read by nothing at all: every other check
    used the derived candidate list, so moving it changed nothing observable.
    There are now three statements of where the answer is and all three must
    agree.

